    "test-argv": {
      "host": "console",
      "title": "PuTTY split_into_argv (self-checking)",
      "_doc": "The smallest thing in the tree that is still PuTTY: its own test for the Windows command-line splitter. It links only `utils`, so it exercises the WHOLE chain -- CMake resolution, the preprocessor, 100-odd translation units, the linker, the runtime -- without needing one GDI call. Its value is that it CHECKS ITSELF: it re-implements Windows' quoting rules and asserts against a table, so a pass is a statement about behaviour, not about compiling.",
      "_define": "CMake puts TEST on ONE FILE -- target_compile_definitions(... PRIVATE TEST) -- and the difference matters: half a dozen units in utils/ carry their own `#ifdef TEST` main(), so defining it program-wide produced eleven duplicate external definitions including six mains. unitDefines is how PRISM says 'this file compiles differently', which is how real builds work.",
      "include": [
        "windows",
        "charset",
        "terminal",
        "../putty-generated"
      ],
